    22	"""
    23	Design analysis for combo D3/3 + D6/3 + D19/1
    24	==============================================
    25	
    26	a) Concrete behavior and trace
    27	- Source task: a black n x n square slides through a blue river toward a same-sized red square.
    28	  If the n x n placement graph reaches the red top-left placement, black covers red; otherwise
    29	  black stops at the farthest reachable placement and the start cell turns blue.
    30	- This combo changes three stages at once.
    31	  1. D19/1 removes reachable training instances entirely, so every generated sample contains a
    32	     real chokepoint narrower than n and the mover always halts upstream.
    33	  2. D3/3 inverts the trail rendering: every departed placement is repainted to background green,
    34	     not river blue, so the output shows a carved-away travel range.
    35	  3. D6/3 would erase mover and target together on contact, but D19 suppresses that branch in all
    36	     generated examples. The solver still supports it for robustness, yet the dataset's visible rule
    37	     is uniform: move until blocked, erase the traversed river behind the block.
    38	- Small trace with n=2:
    39	  input: black 2x2 at one end, red 2x2 downstream, a blue river with one 1-pixel neck.
    40	  source output: start becomes blue, black moves to the last wide-enough 2x2 placement, red stays.
    41	  combo output: start and every intermediate 2x2 placement become green; only the final blocked
    42	  black square remains, and red still stays because the destination is unreachable.
    43	
    44	b) Complexity calibration
    45	- "Always stuck" is only physically possible for n >= 2, so the generator forbids n=1.
    46	- The reverse-erasure effect is easy to miss if the mover advances by only one trivial step or if
    47	  only the original black square changes. The generator therefore requires genuine motion and also
    48	  checks that at least n former river pixels become background in the output.
    49	- A single chokepoint keeps the scene legible. Multiple gates would make the erased range harder to
    50	  interpret and introduce unnecessary ambiguity about which bottleneck matters.
    51	
    52	c) Implicit assumption audit
    53	- Solid-square detection still assumes the two salient non-background objects are monochromatic and
    54	  axis-aligned; the generator preserves that.
    55	- The solver still identifies mover/destination by the original palette convention after grouping by
    56	  size. This is acceptable because the generator keeps exactly one black and one red square of the
    57	  chosen size, so no new invisible tie-breaker is introduced.
    58	- The source solver only needed the final target. D3 breaks that assumption because output painting
    59	  now depends on the full path, so this combo reconstructs a predecessor chain from BFS.
    60	- D19 lets main() simplify the intended branch to "farthest reachable placement", but D6 adds a
    61	  reachable-contact behavior. The combined solver keeps both branches coherent: erase departed
    62	  placements first, then either leave the final blocked mover or erase the destination on contact.
    63	
    64	d) Placement legality
    65	- The generator builds the river first, then carves exactly one chokepoint while preserving pixel
